                         OS9 ViewGIF by Vaughn Cato
                                Version 2.0
                            Formal Documentation

* Parts of this documentation that were added or changed in this version are
marked with an asterisk (*).

  Viewgif is a public domain program for OS9 on the Tandy Color Computer 3.
It was written to provide a better display for GIF pictures.  Viewgif offers
many options in displaying a GIF picture.  By using these options along with
the built-in features of the program, very good representations of GIF
pictures may be produced even for pictures made for more advanced graphics
hardware.

  Viewgif analyzes the colors needed for the GIF picture to find the best
color combinations for the CoCo.  Two mutually exclusive factors are involved
in the analysis.  The dithering factor determines how much difference between
the color desired and the color the CoCo can produce is needed before
dithering is used.  With a dithering factor of 42, if there is any difference
dithering will be used.  With a dithering factor of 0, dithering will never
be used.  The color tolerance used determines the maximum difference between
the color desired and a CoCo color that will be accepted.  If I have
calculated correctly, the maximum needed should be 42, which means any color
plus or minus 42 intensity levels (from a range of 0-255) will be accepted.
  Dithering works in the following way:
    If, for example the color desired is half way between two possible CoCo
colors, then one of the two CoCo colors will be displayed at random when
that color is desired, each color having a 50% chance of being used.  If the
desired color is 3/4 of the way between two possible CoCo colors, then the
farther color will have a 25% chance of being used and the closer color a
75% chance.
  The randomness is somewhat biased depending on what pixel is being
displayed.  The bias is that the upper color will be more likely on even
pixels on even lines and odd pixels on odd lines, and the lower colors will
be more likely on odd pixels on even lines and even pixels on odd lines.
This will produce a checker board type pattern of dithered pixels in the
picture, which is the best possible when the desired color is exactly half
way between two CoCo colors.
  A 640 resolution screen will always be used if the number of palettes
required is 4 or less.  A 320 resolution screen will be used otherwise.
Because of this, any picture using only shades of one color (ie. black and
white) will always be in 640 resolution.
* As of version 2.0, a new feature has been added which I call flickering.
This is a simple technique which involves making two seperate pictures with
seperate palettes and then rapidly flipping between the two windows.  When
this is in effect, the number of available colors increases from 64 to 343,
and the number of colors available at one time goes from 16 up to a
theoretical 128 (most pictures seem to average 30-40).  There is a noticable
flicker in most pictures, but it is not especially annoying.

  The following are options for viewgif.  The options a,r,s,i,n, and p can
  be precede other options after a -.  For example, -ars and -srm2 are
  accptable and will be interpreted as -a -r -s and -s -r -m2 respectively.

* -d[[=]<dfactor>]
     This option allows the user to specify the maximum dithering factor to
     be used.  In some pictures, the dithering affect can be more harmful
     than helpful.  If the dfactor parameter is omitted, 0 will be used.
     Color tolerance can be interpreted as a negative dfactor.

  -a
     The -a option will make the resulting height of the picture be an even
     division of the original picture.  This can be helpful on some pictures
     that have been pre-dithered.  On those pictures, without this option,
     the picture will have horizontal bands of brighter and darker pixels.
     Pictures up to 200 pixels vertically will have a 1:1 ratio of pixels.
     Pictures 201 to 400 pixels vertically will have a 2:1 ratio of pixels
     and so forth.  Without this option, the picture will be made to fit
     exactly within 192 pixels vertically.

  -u[[=]<filename>]
     Many pictures, especially those with more than 32 colors, will often
     not use all of the colors in the color map privided with the picture.
     The unused colors are often random.  These unused colors can cause the
     viewer to have to sacrifice color "nearness" to allocate CoCo colors
     for these colors.  To help avoid this problem, a color usage table
     can be produced the specifies exactly which colors were used in the
     picture.  After a picture has been displayed a count of how many colors
     were used in the picture will be given.  If this number is less than
     the maximum colors for that picture, a color usage file should be
     generated.  Specifying the -u option with the color usage filename
     (the picture filename with a .use extension is recommended) will
     generated the color usage file if it does not exist or use it if it
     does.

  -g
  -g[=]2
     A picture displayed in full color loose alot of detail if many varied
     colors are needed.  In this case, converting the picture to a black
     and white picture is desirable.  These pictures are always displayed
     in 640 resolution because of the number of colors needed is always
     4 or less.  -g will determine the brightness of a pixel by averaging
     the brightness of the red,green, and blue components.  -g2 will determine
     the brightness of a pixel by finding the maximum brightness between the
     red, green, and blue components.

  -r
     By default, a 16x16 element random number table is used for speed in
     dithering.  When using this table, patterns can occur in the picture
     than can be displeasing to the eye.  The -r option will make all random
     numbers be generated with a true random number generator so these
     patterns will not occur.  Display speed will be somewhat slower with
     this option however.

  -m[[=]<magfact>]
     The -m option will allow the user to magnify the picture by an integer
     amount.  -m2 will result in picture with twice the normal detail.
     The magnification can range from 1 (normal) to 64.  On a picture of a
     higher resolution than the CoCo can display, when magnified the
     picture will show detail unseen at normal magnification.

  -x[[=]<start x>]
     The -x option allows you to specify where in the picture from left to
     right display will begin.  Start x is given in 1/64 width increments.
     0 (normal) is the left side of the screen, 32 is the middle, and 64
     is the right side.

  -y[[=]<start y>]
     The -y option is for specifying the vertical position to start within
     the picture and works in a similar manner to the -x option.

* -v[[=]<filename>]
     The -v option will allow you to generate a vef format file of the
     picture.  When this option is used, 320 resolution is always used and
     the picture is always displayed, even when the -n option is used.  If
     no filename is given, no error is produced, but no file is generated.
     Using -v without a filename can be used to force a 320 resolution
     picture (for whatever reason).  When flicker is in use, the -v option
     will cause two files to be saved, one with the file name you specified
     and another with the file name and a .2 extension.  You can use viewvef
     to display these "flicker VEFs."

  -s
     The -s option will turn off the printing of any informative information.
     This option is automatically used if the -c option is used.

  -i
     The -i option prints extended information about the picture, specifically
     the color map for the picture, the resulting CoCo color map, and the
     translation table produced for the picture.  The -c option overrides
     this option.

  -n
     The -n option disables displaying the picture.  If the -n option is used
     and no color usage table is being generated and no vef file is to be
     generated the actual picture will not be read.  This option can be used
     if only information about the picture is desired.  The -v option
     overrides this option.

  -p[[=]<seconds>]
     The -p option sets the delay time after a picture has been displayed.
     Normally, a tone is sounded when a picture is completed and the picture
     is displayed until ENTER is pressed.  If the -p option is used the
     tone will not be generated and no key will be required before continuing.

  -c
     The -c option will force the viewer to use the current window to display
     the picture.  The window will be ended and the screen of the appropriate
     type will be set.  This option forces the -s option and will override the
     -i option.

* -f
     The -f option disables flickering.  Pictures will be displayed in the
     same manner as in ViewGIF 1.0.

* -b[=]<britefac>
     The -b option allows you to specify a brightness factor for the picture.
     The brightness factor is a number ranging from 1 to 16 where 1 is very
     dim and 16 is normal brightness.

   Hints:
     If bands of brighter and darker horizontal lines appear in the picture,
     try using the -a option.  If the number of colors used is less than
     the maximum available (this information is printed after a picture has
     been displayed) try using a color usage file with the -u option.  If the
     picture lacks detail, try using the -g or -g2 options to make a black
     and white version of the picture.  If undesirable patterns occur in the
*    picture, try using the -r option.  If the dithering factor is less than
     21, for flickered pictures, or 42 for non-flickered pictures, then
     decreasing the brightness with the -b option can improve the quality
     of the picture.

*  Notes:
     A picture that uses flicker will take 2 to 3 times as long to analyze
     than with the flicker off.  The analysis stage has been improved somewhat
     in that it will check to see if the best quality will work first and
     not analyze any furthur if it will.  Because of this, if you specify
     a dithering factor with the -d option that is less than or equal to
     the best dithering factor for a picture (this information is given
     after analysis is complete) the analysis time will be reduced
     significantly, so keeping track of dithering factors for each picture
     can be helpful.  I have found that it helps greatly to have a directory
     with usage files and another directory with the gif files and then
     have script files for each picture which will display the picture
     with the best parameters you have found.

     When a picture that uses flicker is being displayed, it will slowly
     switch back and forth between colors.  Don't worry, this is NOT the
     "flicker" effect.

